// KNOWN-DIVERGENT, recorded as a defect and NOT as correct behaviour.
// NaN is reachable without a literal: a Float multiplied past the
// largest finite value is inf, and inf - inf is NaN. Every ordering
// comparison against NaN is false, so a comparator built from < and >
// answers 0 for NaN against anything and is no longer a consistent
// total order.
//
// FIVE elements, NaN at index 1, and the arrangement matters. The
// four-element version this fixture used to carry recorded only that
// NaN MOVED, which reads as a cosmetic ordering question. At five
// elements the real defect is visible: on the two Python paths the
// CLEAN elements come out in the order 2.0, 1.0, 3.0, 4.0, which is
// not sorted. One NaN silently destroys the ordering of data that has
// nothing wrong with it, and the Wasm merge sort corrupts differently
// (it happens not to corrupt on this input, which is itself the
// divergence). A sort that returns unsorted clean data is not a weaker
// guarantee, it is a false one.
//
// This stays DEFERRED because the comparator here is the USER's.
// Where the COMPILER supplies the comparator (List.sorted) it supplies
// a total one, so sorted() cannot reach this state at all; see
// docs/stdlib.md and the sorted rows in list_methods.capa.

fun main(stdio: Stdio)
    var big = 1.0
    var i = 0
    while i < 400
        big = big * 10.0
        i = i + 1
    let nan = big - big
    let xs = [2.0, nan, 3.0, 1.0, 4.0]
    let s = xs.sorted_by(fun (a, b) => if a < b then 0 - 1 else (if a > b then 1 else 0))
    stdio.println("${s.fold("", fun (acc, x) => acc + "${x}|")}")
